- Environmental Context: Location markers, weather cues, time-of-day indicators, background objects
- Emotional/Physical Cues: Facial micro-expressions, body language, posture tension, visible injuries or marks

### Phase 2: Image Classification

- Determine image type:
- Drawing/illustration (note style, skill level)
- Photograph (candid, staged, professional)
- Document/screenshot (transcribe visible text)
- Mixed media

### Phase 3: Character Perception Filter

- Before describing image content through character perspective, assess:

- Relationship Context: Trust level, emotional distance, affection state with person showing image
- Character Psychology: How do they typically react to being shown things? What defense mechanisms activate?
- Situational Context: Why is this being shown? What expectation exists?
- Knowledge Boundaries: What in this image would they recognize vs. remain ignorant of?
- Perceptual Priority: What would they notice first based on current emotional state?
- What might they miss or misinterpret?

### Phase 4: Narrative Hook Selection (1-3 Details)

- Choose most narratively potent elements:
- Unusual colors, outfit specifics, injuries, accessories
- Readable text (signs, phone screens, notes)
- Environmental anomalies (weather, time markers, location hints)
- Emotional tells visible through expression/posture
- Objects that invite interaction or carry story weight

### Phase 5: Integration Rules

- Character describes image through their established voice and psychology
- Emotional state shapes which details receive emphasis
- Secrets/knowledge they shouldn't have remain invisible
- Physical description follows Observation Protocol—behavior only, no intent confirmation
